okay so if you're a 90s baby like me  then ghostbusters was probably something  that you saw in all aspects of your  childhood whether it was the films the  cartoon tv show or even just the weird  and wonderful toys the franchise had a  massive impact growing up i was obsessed  with it and i remember as a kid being  terrified of ego in ghostbusters too  just something about a painting of a man  looking menacing was absolutely  horrifying and it constantly made me wet  the bed sorry stay up late at night  unable to sleep unfortunately though  after the first two movies released the  franchise just seemed to fizzle out and  though i didn't really know why this was  as a kid there were a lot of things  going on behind the scenes ghostbusters  3 was pretty much stuck in development  hell for several years and though we got  a video game that was supposed to work  as a continuation of the story things  just didn't feel the same in 2014 harold  ramirez sadly passed away and when we  got the ghostbusters reboot in 2016 it  felt like that was the end in terms of a  true sequel ever been created now that  movie was pretty much completely  disconnected from the previous two films  and though it had some actors from the  original appearing cameo roles they were  completely different characters because  of that it just felt like ghostbusters  would remain as a product of its time  the original 1984 film is still a  classic but after seeing the dismal 2016  reboot i thought that was the final nail  in the coffin however there was still  some hope after death or after life of  the franchisor i don't know but  there is a pun in there somewhere now  this came when sony announced that they  were creating a sequel to the original  two movies that would follow aegon's  daughter and also her children stranger  things clearly took a lot of inspiration  from ghostbusters and when finn wolfhard  was announced it seemed like perfect  casting add to that mckenna grace who's  one of the best child actors working  right now paul rudd and carrie [ __ ] and  it did seem like they were attempting to  do something beyond just rehashing what  we'd seen before now ghostbusters  afterlife is definitely from the school  of force awakens when it comes to  basically remaking major elements of the  first film but i think the casting  choices and characters do help to  differentiate it the film clearly leans  into nostalgia and though it is a bit  memo berries at times i think that the  movie does enough things with its core  cast where it doesn't feel like you're  just re-watching the first film all over  again now because of these new casting  announcements different story settings  and of course the death of reimus it did  feel like we wouldn't get a true reunion  on screen however the movie actually  ends with the original group showing up  in order to take down gozai and save us  from the evil ghost that the entity was  about to unleash on the world it's such  a powerful scene and seeing the force  standing together on screen again brings  every emotion flooding back from your  childhood now it's something that i  never thought we'd get even when  watching the movie in the opening of the  film we see aegon fleeing from shandor  mining co and though it's a cool little  tribute on my first watch i felt like  they clearly had to hold back egon is  heavily basked in shadow obscured from  view and though it was a nice way to  start the movie i thought it was the  closest we'd get to seeing him appear in  the film throughout his ghost someone  acts as a guide to his granddaughter  phoebe but it's clear that they're just  implying he's there rather than actually  showing him now this is understandable  as well as over the last decade or so  we've had several characters return from  the dead through the use of cgi whether  it's peter cushing in rogue one carrie  fisher and rise of skywalker or even  paul walker and fast and the furious  though these have helped with the story  something about them just always felt a  bit off more times than not bringing an  actor back to life is distracting and in  the words of malcolm from jurassic park  yeah but your scientists were so  preoccupied with whether or not they  could they didn't stop to think if they  should  thus when i watched afterlife for the  first time i could understand why the  opening was the way that it was it at  least set things up for the future and  the pke meter that was dropped on the  floor would be somewhat of a passing of  the torch from the old to the new now  the film culminates much in the same way  that the events in the opening happened  gozar travels to egon's farmhouse and  the heroes attempt to trap it things  don't go to plan though and the vengeful  spirit easily overpowers the family  however at the last second ray peter and  winston arrived to save the day seeing  the three united on screen was one of  the biggest moments i think i've had in  a theater over the last couple of years  and that's not just because the pandemic  basically killed cinema had toby and  andrew not showing up a couple of months  later then i think that this would have  been the biggest moment for characters  returning this year and it's difficult  not to well up when you see them coming  back well not me  i wasn't crying you you were crying now  just hearing that bass note drop in as  the pro unpack powers up is enough to  shake your tv and it's easily one of the  best sound effects that we've ever seen  in cinema  he really felt the good guys had  returned and that they were finally  gonna put an end to gosei however goza  easily beats the trio and then all that  was left was phoebe to fight it off  standing there blasting the proton pack  at gozer it started to slowly walk  towards the young girl completely  overpowering her but you should never  give up hope and when all felt lost a  ghostly hand came in and helped phoebe  to steady her aim the music and sound  effects completely cut out and there  standing alongside his granddaughter was  aegon back in his old ghostbusters  overalls this gave the other original  members time to fire their weapons too  and the scene culminates with all four  finally standing side by side in a  reunion that we never thought we were  gonna get i don't know how they managed  to perfect the cg to this point that  reymus looks flawless but it's difficult  to even tell that this is an effect over  the years reymus sadly had health issues  and he contracted a disease that left  him unable to walk however what the  scene does is that it imagines reimus as  how the character egon would have aged  but it also brings back the actor in  somewhat his prime when we imagine the  afterlife and being reunited with those  we love we often think about them at  their best and not how we knew them  before we said goodbye having rey missed  back from the afterlife not only tied  into the title but also gave the actors  a chance to say farewell in real life  raymus and murray fell out during  groundhog day which the former directed  the pair didn't speak for more than 20  years but just before his death murray  ended up reaching out to ramus and the  pair buried the hatchet murray went to  his house and though remus was unable to  talk through most of their conversations  over several hours the pair went back  and forth recounting their happy  memories murray ended up paying tribute  to the actor at the 86 academy awards  and it's nice knowing that the two  reconciled before reimus passed away  when someone dies we often regret the  things we said and the way that we acted  with them i feel this is somewhat  reflected in the final things that the  actors say to his ghost winston says he  should have called and that he misses  him which i think is a sentiment we can  all relate to when thinking of those  that have passed on wisely the creative  team chose not to have egon speak either  and this somewhat stops the scene from  feeling like there are any elements that  could take away from the sincerity of  what's on screen in the end the  characters get to say goodbye to not  only egon but also reimus which is  something that we must all learn to do  he finally gets to build a bridge with  the daughter that he left behind and the  grandchildren he didn't get to spend  much time with callie hugs some as he  fades into the ether and we see the  other characters crying which hey if  this doesn't set you off i don't know  what will cried about five times when i  was writing this script now he vanishes  into the story night and the scene ends  with the words for harold making this  one of the best send-offs that we could  have got we close out on ecto-1 cruising  through the streets and though we get  some fun post-credits scenes i feel like  the goodbye to harold is the perfect way  to end the movie now i'd of course love  to hear your thoughts on the scene so  make sure you comment below and let me  know we're in a competition right now  and giving away three copies of an mc  box of your choice on the 30th of  january and all you have to do to be on  the chance of winning is like the video  make sure you subscribe with  notifications on and drop a comment  below with your thoughts on the film we  pick the comments at random at the end  of the month and the winners of the last  one on screen right now so that's you  then message me on twitter heavyspoilers  if you want some else to watch then make  sure you check out our breakdown of  spiderman no way home which will be  linked on screen right now we talk about  the ending and how much we love it so  definitely go head over there right  after this if not then thanks for  sticking through the video i've been  paul i'll see you on the next one take  care peace
 

